                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "PowBall Renaissance is an excellent 2D retro breakout with  two modes of play;  i.e. single player & local  multiplayer.  There are several power ups that are activated by breaking bricks, and some of which can also be purchased via the shop that is accessible at the end of every level.  Some  of the  bricks you destroy turn into collectable power ups; however these have a limited time span, so you must hit them as soon as possible to gain the power up.  Before they disappear they will start to flash; giving you some warning of their demise.\n\n\nTo use the shop you have to collect crystals (called alchemites) that appear when you destroy bricks,  enemies, asteroids or space saucers.   Each crystal collected is worth ten alchemites.\n\nThe local multiplayer mode can be played against bots or up to two other players;  or, you can play cooperatively.  If the cooperative mode is switched off, it  will require each player to have a ball in play and turns on friendly fire when using either the rockets, lasers and bullets\n\nThere are three control options when playing multiplayer using the Arrow keys, WASD & Mouse; plus a fourth  if you set up a game controller. \n\nThere are three difficulty levels i.e., easy, normal & hard.  The  difference  between each appears to be in the speed of the game;  ([b]I have yet to confirm this[/b]).   Each of the modes has four level sets, i.e. Classic, Renaissance, Retro & Custom.  The Custom levels are created using the in game level editor.  I've not completed any of the level sets yet, nor used the level editor, however, there at at least 300  pre-made levels to keep you occupied for a reasonable amount of game play time.  As for the custom levels; I don't know if the set is limitless or confined to 100 levels.  I also do not know how easy it is to use the level editor yet; however, after briefly checking it out, and having used many other breakout level editors, it appears to be standard fare and should be relatively easy to figure out.  \n\nWhen you first start the game,  there's a very basic tutorial that indicates what you should hit, collect or avoid when playing.\n\nYou can set up to five different profiles, but you can only select one level set for each profile; you cannot swap between level sets in the same profile.  If you wish to change to a different level set from the same profile you have to wipe your progress via the main menu.   Progress is sequential and you cannot restart a previous level from the same profile unless you've lost all of your ships; in which case you have to start from level one again.  \n\nOnce you've earned enough alchemites, you can purchase several different items via the shop that include up to 50 standard issue mining balls, 99 shells for the cannon, 99 fuel cells for the plasma cannon, 50 high explosive unguided fragmentation rockets and a limited number of extra mining ships (the bat).  When in standard mode i.e. when no weapon is selected,  each time you press LMB it will launch another mining ball; you can have as many mining balls in play as you have in your store; however, once you get more than three or four in play, it becomes very difficult to keep them all in play. There is an achievement for launching 50 mining balls in the same level; however, it's quite difficult to get this achievement unless you allow most balls to miss the ship on their first bounce back because it's more than likely you'll have destroyed every brick before you've managed to launch all 50 mining balls.\n\nYou can also mod the game;  there's access to an help screen via the main menu to start modding the game; however, the developer does recommend you have some technical knowledge of modding if you wish to  mod the game\n\nIf you don't own a mouse with sensitivity controls there's also the ability to adjust the mouse sensitivity from 1% to 100%. The default setting is 50%.  From the options menu you can adjust the sound quality, toggle screen shake,  as well as enemy outlines, tips, & controller rumble.  To change the mouse sensitivity while in game use PgUp & PgDn.  To change weapons, use [b]RMB[/b] to scroll between the standard ship, ballistic cannon, plasma cannon or rockets.  To fire each weapon or additional mining balls use [b]LMB[/b] and to move use the [b]Left & Right axis[/b] of the mouse.  If you are playing single player, the control option defaults to the mouse.  From the options menu there appears to be an option to select a game controller as an alternative; however, because I don't use controllers, I've not been able to check this out.\n\n\nThere are 20 Steam achievements or 60 level set achievements to collect; i.e, 20 for each of the non custom sets.  These 20 achievements are duplicated for each level set; though I haven't checked to see if they are available for the custom set.  \n\nAchievements  are awarded for:\n\nCollecting alchemites\nDestroying enemies\nHaving barely any shield left\nClearing a level quickly\nDestroying  asteroids \n......Plus more \n\nRegarding the shield, some enemies fire weapons at your mining ship; unfortunately, too many hits will destroy your ship, so do avoid them if you can.   Also, when side swiping asteroids and/or  enemy space ships or other obstacles (which is necessary to gain one of the achievements as well as to create more alchemites).  Unfortunately,  too many hits will also destroy your ship.  \n \nYou can check the progress of the level set you are playing for each of the five profiles via the progress button in the main menu.  This shows the achievements you have gained for each level set as well as what ammunition you have left, the number of lives (ships) and mining balls left, as well as the name of the level set; however, you are only awarded the Steam achievement once.  You can wipe the progress from any profile if you wish to start the set from the beginning.\n \nThere are leader boards for the global community as well as your friends, and you can check to see how you are fairing with respect to your progress at attaining the various achievements.\n\nPowBall Renaissance  is a remake of the original PowBall by Anthony Hamilton, which  has been freeware for  many years. You can download the original from Caiman Games [url=http://www.caiman.us/scripts/fw/f633.html][b]HERE[/b][/url]; however all the  levels from the original are included with  Powball: Renaissance; still, you can play the original without having to boot up Steam and you'll see it in it's original retro graphic, (Yeah!).  \n\nIf you are not happy clicking on the above link via Steam, (I've been hacked in the past due to clicking a seemingly friendly link), you can find the game by Googling [b]PowBall[/b] & [b]Caiman Games[/b].  Or just Caiman Games, then clicking on the Breakout links via the left hand panel.  There are 16 pages of free breakouts, each page listing 15 games.  There's also a 3D Breakout section with 30 free 3D breakouts.\n\n[b]Conclusion:[/b]\n\nPowBall Renaissance has all the elements of a playable and decent 2D breakout, with over 300 levels to  beat.  It's ideal for new and experienced players alike, thanks to the three difficulty levels and the level editor.    The game doesn't bring anything new to the table that I've not seen before.  I've purchased literally hundreds of these breakout arcade games over the many decades since I first played the original breakout that was \n an offshoot of the  original Pong game.  Breakout was created by Steve Wozniak and published by Atari back in the mid 1970's.  \n\nIf you love the old breakout/arkanoid games from the 70's & 80's, Powball: Renaissance will definitely scratch  that itch some more.  \n\nFor the measly price of £3.99 (~$5), when not on sale, PowBall Renaissance is in my opinion a steal and worth buying at full price (if only to support the developer).",
